<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Privacy manifest. Required at submission since spring 2024.

	Proxima collects nothing. The phone downloads a timetable file from a URL
	the user types in — a public open-data feed published by a transit agency —
	compiles the stops the user picked, and sends the result to the watch over
	WatchConnectivity. The watch never touches the network at all.

	Worth being explicit about, because a departure board is exactly the kind
	of app people expect to be tracking them: there is no Core Location code,
	no nearby-stops feature, no analytics, and no server belonging to this app.
	The agency's web server sees an ordinary HTTP request for a file it
	publishes to everyone, and that is the only outbound traffic.

	NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is therefore an empty array rather than absent.
	Both mean the same thing to App Store Connect, but an empty array is a
	statement and a missing key is indistinguishable from an oversight.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
